    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      Three things, stated plainly.
      <br><br>
      <b>1. The dial's accepted digits.</b> Three crops of the dial at different seconds read it as 2 / 0 / 1, as 9 / 1 / 5 and as 4 / 0 / 5 — the digits are small green seven-segment numbers on coloured segments and they cycle as the player turns them, so no single frame is authoritative. The <b>colour mapping</b> from the tulip planters (red 7, magenta 2, yellow 5) is solid and was read twice; the dial's final state is not.
      <br><br>
      <b>2. The exact hex-board solution.</b> The rule (match every touching lobe) is clear from the frames, but the board's final orientation is never held still long enough to transcribe tile by tile. This page teaches the rule rather than printing a layout.
      <br><br>
      <b>3. What the cardboard box contained.</b> The drawer opens at about 7:02 and a cardboard box is visible inside, but the item is never shown in a labelled close-up. We do not name it.
    </div>
